CAT = "rbtAssess"; SEG = "SASM"
Q = [
# B.1 preference assessments
("A technician sets up a paired-stimulus preference assessment with 5 snacks, as the supervisor directed. How should the snacks be presented?",
 "Each with every other snack", ["Each snack alone, once", "All five in one array", "Each with the favorite only"],
 "A paired-stimulus assessment presents every item with every other item, two at a time, so items can be ranked by how often each is chosen. Showing all five at once is a multiple-stimulus format."),
("During an MSWO assessment, a client keeps choosing whatever item is on the far left of the array. The technician should:",
 "Rotate positions each trial", ["Keep items in fixed places", "Count left picks as top choices", "Remove the far left item"],
 "Rotating positions keeps a side bias from looking like a preference. Counting left-side picks as preferences would rank items by where they sat, not by what the client wants."),
("A client screams whenever items are taken away between choice trials. The supervisor wants a preference assessment that never removes items. Which fits?",
 "A free-operant assessment", ["An MSWO assessment", "A paired-stimulus assessment", "An MSW assessment"],
 "In a free-operant assessment all items stay available and the technician records time spent with each, so nothing is removed. MSWO, paired-stimulus and MSW all take items away between trials."),
("A brief MSWO before today's session ranks chips first, though last week the tablet ranked first. For today's session, the technician should use:",
 "Chips as today's top item", ["The tablet, as ranked before", "The item the parent suggests", "Both items as one reward"],
 "Preferences change, and the brief assessment shows what the client wants right now. Using last week's ranking is the error that frequent brief assessments are meant to prevent."),
("A client always picks cookies first in preference assessments, but in one run right after lunch picks cookies last. The most likely reason is:",
 "Satiation after eating", ["Deprivation after eating", "Cookies became punishers", "A position bias in the array"],
 "Eating just before the assessment reduces the value of food, which is satiation. Deprivation would make cookies more valuable, not less."),
("In a 10-minute free-operant assessment, a client goes to the blocks first and plays with them for 4 minutes, looks at books for 1 minute, and plays music for 5 minutes. Which item shows the highest preference?",
 "Music, with the most time", ["Blocks, since chosen first", "Books, the newest item", "Blocks and music, tied"],
 "Free-operant assessments rank items by time engaged, so music at 5 minutes ranks highest. Being approached first is not the measure in this format."),
("The plan for a paired-stimulus assessment defines a selection as touching an item. A client looks at and smiles at the drum but touches the bell. The technician records:",
 "The bell as selected", ["The drum as selected", "Both as selected", "No selection that trial"],
 "Data follow the written definition, which is touching. Smiling at the drum may look like interest, but it is not a selection under this definition."),
# B.2 skill assessments
("During a curriculum-based skill assessment, a client gives a wrong answer to an item the technician has seen the client do correctly at home. The technician should:",
 "Score it as it occurred", ["Score it correct from history", "Repeat it until he gets it", "Prompt, then score it correct"],
 "Assessment data record what happens under the assessment conditions. The technician can mention the home observation to the supervisor, but scoring it correct from history makes the results inaccurate."),
("After the RBT helps run a developmental skill assessment, a mother asks what the results mean for her child's school placement. The RBT should:",
 "Refer her to the supervisor", ["Explain the likely placement", "Share the raw scores with her", "Say the scores look typical"],
 "Interpreting assessment results is the supervisor's job, not the RBT's. Explaining placement or judging the scores goes beyond the RBT's role."),
("For a social skills assessment, the supervisor asks the RBT to observe a client at recess. Which record is most useful?",
 "Count of peer initiations", ["Notes on how shy he seems", "His view of recess in words", "A rating of his popularity"],
 "A count of observable social behavior gives the supervisor data to judge strengths and deficits. Shyness and popularity are judgments, not observations."),
("While helping with a skill assessment, the RBT sees a client read labels well above the level the assessment is testing. The RBT should:",
 "Note it for the supervisor", ["Add a reading goal herself", "Skip the remaining reading items", "Raise the scores to match"],
 "Strengths matter to the assessment, and the supervisor decides what to do with them. Adding a goal or changing scores would go beyond the RBT's role."),
("A skill assessment protocol says to give each instruction once and score the response. A client does not respond, and the RBT is tempted to say it again. The RBT should:",
 "Give it once, as written", ["Repeat it until he responds", "Add a gesture prompt once", "Reword it more simply"],
 "Standard administration lets results be compared with the criteria. Repeating, prompting or rewording changes the assessment and can inflate the score."),
# B.3 functional assessment
("A client hits after the teacher says \"Math time,\" and the teacher then sends the client to the hallway. In ABC data, the consequence is:",
 "Being sent to the hallway", ["The teacher saying \"Math time\"", "Hitting the teacher", "The math worksheet itself"],
 "The consequence is what happens right after the behavior, here the trip to the hallway. The instruction to start math came before the hit, so it is the antecedent."),
("A week of ABC data shows that most of a client's hits follow a work demand and are followed by the demand being removed. Asked by the supervisor, which function does this suggest?",
 "Escape from demands", ["Access to attention", "Access to tangibles", "Automatic reinforcement"],
 "Behavior that follows demands and ends them points to escape (negative reinforcement). Attention would show a pattern of hits followed by someone talking to or looking at the client."),
("The supervisor gives the RBT an ABC form with checkboxes for preset antecedents and consequences to tick after each incident. This format is:",
 "Structured ABC recording", ["Narrative ABC recording", "A functional analysis", "Scatterplot recording"],
 "Structured (checklist) ABC recording uses preset categories. Narrative ABC recording describes each incident in the observer's own words."),
("A scatterplot shows that most of a client's self-injury happens between 2 and 3 p.m. on school days. The RBT should:",
 "Report it to the supervisor", ["Move work out of that hour", "Change the plan for that hour", "Stop data in the other hours"],
 "Descriptive data help the supervisor form hypotheses and decide on changes. Rescheduling work or changing the plan on her own goes beyond the RBT's role."),
("During a functional analysis run by the supervisor, problem behavior is high in the alone condition and low in the attention, escape and play conditions. This most suggests:",
 "Automatic reinforcement", ["Attention as the function", "Escape as the function", "No function was shown"],
 "Behavior that persists when the client is alone with no social consequences suggests it is maintained by automatic reinforcement. The low rates in the social conditions argue against attention or escape."),
("In the tangible condition of a functional analysis, the RBT removes a preferred toy as directed. When the client hits, the RBT should:",
 "Give the toy back briefly", ["Block and keep the toy away", "Give attention, not the toy", "Present a work demand"],
 "In the tangible condition, problem behavior is followed by brief access to the item, to test whether access maintains it. Giving attention instead would test a different function."),
("In the attention condition of a functional analysis, the RBT is told to read a magazine. The client says \"Look at me\" with no problem behavior. The RBT should:",
 "Keep reading, as planned", ["Praise the appropriate request", "Give brief attention now", "End the condition early"],
 "In the attention condition, attention is given only after the target problem behavior. Praising the request, though it would be good teaching elsewhere, would contaminate the condition."),
("During a functional analysis session, a client's head hitting reaches the termination criterion written in the plan. The RBT should:",
 "End the session per the plan", ["Finish the session's time first", "Wait for the supervisor's signal", "Switch to the play condition"],
 "Termination criteria protect the client, so the session ends as soon as one is met. Finishing the time or waiting for a signal keeps the client at risk."),
]
